                "entry_id": "e1039116-9f35-3eaf-afa8-105f4f4057c8",
                "word": "aestheticism",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "5324ecfe-7b2f-4c53-ac4d-51b5039eb35a",
                        "entry_id": "e1039116-9f35-3eaf-afa8-105f4f4057c8",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "aestheticisms"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "cce9e1e2-d434-3302-9d72-7140c03c07c8",
                        "entry_id": "e1039116-9f35-3eaf-afa8-105f4f4057c8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A doctrine which holds aesthetics or beauty as the highest ideal or most basic standard."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4b4fb1c-34ff-490d-9793-2a14d0017f8c",
                                "sense_id": "cce9e1e2-d434-3302-9d72-7140c03c07c8",
                                "text": "He went over his canvases with disgust and anger, unable to see virtue in any one of them. Even his sacred Oyster Girl went back on him. The creature of a vitiated æstheticism, he could only suppose that conceit had played an abominable trick on his eyesight."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4426527-1ef6-4bbd-b122-2b20b7f74e7a",
                                "sense_id": "cce9e1e2-d434-3302-9d72-7140c03c07c8",
                                "text": "Born the most sensitive of children into an unhappy family that misreared and misschooled him, Rilke recoiled into introspectiveness and dilletante^([sic]) aestheticism, and long remained there; the world, or outwardness, was what had hurt him, was the enemy."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
